      {
        "id": "p21-p5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What is the probability that Bob has two aces?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac16$",
          "worked": "The six equally likely hands are\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}&\\{A\\spadesuit,2\\spadesuit\\},\\quad\\{A\\spadesuit,A\\heartsuit\\},\\quad\\{A\\spadesuit,2\\heartsuit\\},\\\\&\\{2\\spadesuit,A\\heartsuit\\},\\quad\\{2\\spadesuit,2\\heartsuit\\},\\quad\\{A\\heartsuit,2\\heartsuit\\}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nExactly one hand contains both aces, so $P(\\text{two aces})=\\frac16$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-p5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Bob tells Mary “I have an ace”. Explain why this increases the probability of Bob having two aces, and find this new probability.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac15$.\n\nThe hand containing both twos is ruled out, and the two-ace hand is still possible.",
          "worked": "The information rules out $\\{2\\spadesuit,2\\heartsuit\\}$. The other five hands remain equally likely and one contains both aces. There is still one favourable hand, now out of five instead of six.\n\n$$P(\\text{two aces}\\mid\\text{at least one ace})=\\frac15.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-p5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Bob now then tells Mary “I have an ace of spades”. Do you think telling Mary what suit his ace is will change the likelihood of Bob having two aces? Justify your answer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes, it increases. The ace of spades rules out some of the possible hands, and the two-ace hand remains possible.",
          "worked": "Two of the five hands from part (b) contain the ace of hearts and a two. Knowing that Bob has the ace of spades rules out both of these hands. The two-ace hand remains among fewer equally likely hands, so its probability increases.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-p5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Calculate the probability that Bob has two aces, given this additional information, and determine whether your answer from part (c) was correct.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac13$.\n\nThis is greater than $\\frac15$, confirming the prediction that the probability increases.",
          "worked": "The equally likely hands containing the ace of spades are\n\n$$\\{A\\spadesuit,2\\spadesuit\\},\\quad\\{A\\spadesuit,A\\heartsuit\\},\\quad\\{A\\spadesuit,2\\heartsuit\\}.$$ \n\nOnly one of these hands has two aces.\n\n$$P(\\text{two aces}\\mid\\text{ace of spades})=\\frac13.$$ \n\nSince $\\frac13>\\frac15$, the probability increases, as predicted in part (c).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
